  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Well above normal (summerlike) temperatures today then \nshowers and cooler temps follow for tonight and into early Saturday.\n\nHigh pressure remains offshore will a very mild and humid airmass\nhas established itself across our area. Today will be the warmest\nday of the stretch with low/mid 80s widespread across the area.\nHighs in the upper 70s across the southern Poconos. Dry conditions\nshould prevail through mid-day.\n\nThis will change this afternoon as a cold front will pass across\nthe region this evening and early tonight and will settle over \nDelmarva on Saturday. Scattered showers will develop this afternoon,\nmainly north and west of the I-95 corridor, then spread east tonight.\nThe showers taper off in most areas by Saturday morning, but across\nsouthern Delaware and south NJ, we'll keep some clouds and scattered\nshowers for Saturday as the front becomes nearly stationary over\nthe Mid-Atlantic. Overall, QPF will be less than 1/10 inch but \na few areas closer to 2/10ths of an inch are possible.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled conditions are expected for Sunday and\nSunday night. Rain chances remain about the same as yesterday\nhowever.  \n\nHigh pressure then builds into the region from the north during \nSaturday. Mostly sunny skies for most of the area and partly to \nmostly cloudy skies across Delmarva and extreme southern New \nJersey with seasonable, but slightly below normal temperatures \nwith highs in the upper 60s to around 70 degrees.\n\nAfter that, low pressure develops on that stalled front and passes\ncross the Mid-Atlantic and Delmarva Sunday and Sunday night. The\nprevious fcst has low-end likely POPS for southern Delaware and\nthe 50%-60% chance ribbon runs along the Delaware Valley and up\ninto northern Ocean County. Low chance pops are included now for\nthe southern Poconos and Lehigh Valley. These are perhaps 0-5% \nhigher than this time yesterday. The present forecast QPF totals\nare a bit lighter than previous fcsts. Current totals are a \nhalf-inch across southern DE and 0.1 to 0.2 for the Delaware \nValley. \n\nHigh pressure and a much cooler air mass then builds into the \nregion starting Monday into Tuesday. High temperatures Tuesday\nare only near 60 degrees for much of the region, which is\nupwards of 10 degrees below normal. Some patchy frost is\npossible by Tuesday night into Wednesday morning. Forecast lows\nare in the mid 30s north of I-78, and in the low to mid 40s\nelsewhere.",
